04:59.000 --> 05:03.000
Because, you know, my show
basically starts at the top and
ends at the bottom of the list.

05:03.000 --> 05:08.000
And as I go along, sometimes
find myself changing stuff,
moving stuff around.

05:08.000 --> 05:12.000
It kind of corresponds with what
I'm doing in Megasag, although
there's a lot of manual

05:12.000 --> 05:15.000
intervention there, keeping both
lists synchronized.

05:15.000 --> 05:16.000
Oh, man.

05:16.000 --> 05:21.000
Wouldn't it be nice if Megasag
could import my Omni Outliner
file?

05:21.000 --> 05:23.000
Oh, my goodness.

05:23.000 --> 05:26.000
That would be fucking awesome.

05:26.000 --> 05:31.000
Anyway, it's cool because you
can basically drag and drop MP3s
into it.

05:31.000 --> 05:36.000
Bookmarks for URLs, all this
stuff, and it just becomes my
console, if you will.

05:36.000 --> 05:37.000
Do you hear that?

05:37.000 --> 05:41.000
I got a, that's the radiator
now.

05:41.000 --> 05:44.000
Yeah, at first I thought it was
Roger Smalls going through my
trash again.

05:44.000 --> 05:48.000
But no, it turns out the
radiator at this point has so
much air in it that it sounds

05:48.000 --> 05:52.000
like someone's dragging a milk
cart in the cross, the pavement
out in front of the cottage.

05:52.000 --> 05:54.000
A lot of racking.

05:54.000 --> 05:58.000
I got to find the key to let the
air out.

05:58.000 --> 06:03.000
So yesterday, whoa, big, big,
big, big day.

06:03.000 --> 06:10.000
Went to see my mom, who, first
of all, thank you all so much
for all the wonderful emails

06:10.000 --> 06:15.000
that you've sent me emails of
support, encouragement of hope
of prayer.

06:15.000 --> 06:18.000
It's really, really appreciated.

06:18.000 --> 06:24.000
And I mean, I, I've tried to
respond, I started this morning,
started to respond to,

06:24.000 --> 06:27.000
to just about everyone who sent
something in, there's so much.

06:27.000 --> 06:31.000
I'm sorry if I missed yours, but
I really appreciated this.

06:31.000 --> 06:34.000
Well, we'll get to the whole
cancer thing in a moment.

06:34.000 --> 06:39.000
Let me take the day step by step
because it was quite a, quite a
rollercoaster ride,

06:39.000 --> 06:40.000
actually.

06:40.000 --> 06:43.000
First of all, the wind commander
stayed overnight, so we had an
early start.

06:43.000 --> 06:50.000
Well, when you have two women in
the house, an early start is
basically leaving within 30
minutes of your estimated time
with the part show.

06:50.000 --> 06:51.000
You're doing pretty good.

06:51.000 --> 06:54.000
We drove off to the, to Farrow,
to the airport.

06:54.000 --> 06:57.000
We were there around, I wanted
to be there around 830.

06:57.000 --> 06:58.000
We're there on 9 o'clock.

06:58.000 --> 06:59.000
So I got everything.

06:59.000 --> 07:01.000
We were already pre-fueled.

07:01.000 --> 07:02.000
Everything was ready to go.

07:02.000 --> 07:06.000
I just went up the tower to make
sure we had everything all set.

07:06.000 --> 07:08.000
And our flight plan was filed.

07:08.000 --> 07:14.000
Also just to say hi, you know,
it's a small local airfield to
kind of drop in and be social
for a couple of minutes.

07:14.000 --> 07:16.000
And then we took off.

07:16.000 --> 07:21.000
It wasn't a, it wasn't a
brilliant day, but flying at
what we do.

07:21.000 --> 07:30.000
We were around 3,000 feet was
nice between broken clouds above
us around four and a half
thousand feet and a cloud cover
below us.

07:30.000 --> 07:32.000
Now, I don't have an instrument
rating.

07:32.000 --> 07:34.000
I am training from an instrument
rating.

07:34.000 --> 07:39.000
So we do a lot of work in, in
the plane on these types of
flights, the wind commander and
I.

07:39.000 --> 07:41.000
And he gives me all kinds of
stuff to do.

07:41.000 --> 07:45.000
And now we're not flying on a
visual GPS, you know, with a
nice screen or anything.

07:45.000 --> 07:49.000
None of that stuff is, I do have
a GPS, but it's a very, very
basic system.

07:49.000 --> 07:51.000
Just basically with a text read
out.

07:51.000 --> 07:57.000
But most of the training that
you do for instruments is, well,
guess what, flying on
instruments, which includes
tuning in,

07:57.000 --> 07:59.000
VORs.

07:59.000 --> 08:11.000
So you can basically vector
where you are and being able to
hold altitude and, and your
direction while essentially
being in complete cloud cover,
which is a very weird sensation.

08:11.000 --> 08:13.000
I mean, that's really, we've
talked about this before.

08:13.000 --> 08:15.000
They call it kind of the leans.

08:15.000 --> 08:16.000
Well, you'll be flying.

08:16.000 --> 08:19.000
And if you look outside and, of
course, you see nothing but
white around you.

08:19.000 --> 08:21.000
You know that you're moving, but
you can't see anything.

08:21.000 --> 08:26.000
It becomes like a, a really
interesting Disney type park
ride.

08:27.000 --> 08:35.000
And you'll think, well, wait a
minute, the plane is going right
and I should be, you know, and
you, your body almost starts
leaning left or vice versa.

08:35.000 --> 08:43.000
And you want to turn and then
you look at your instruments and
you see that the attitude of the
airplane is doing something
completely different than what
you thought it was doing.

08:43.000 --> 08:49.000
Now, we have an autopilot in the
machine, but when you're doing
these exercises, really, you
know, doing it all by hand is
what it's about.

08:50.000 --> 08:57.000
So, mostly it was all clear
smooth sailing until we got up
to Rotterdam where the, the
cover was at like 600 feet.

08:57.000 --> 09:02.000
So it was a complete IFR
approach instrument flying
rules, which I did by hand.

09:02.000 --> 09:08.000
And at the same time, it had
kind of a panic-stricken
passenger in the back, Christina
started to freak out because,

09:08.000 --> 09:10.000
well, both the girls had elected
not to wear headsets.

09:10.000 --> 09:15.000
And I think that when you're not
hearing what's going on and
you're not seeing anything
because you look out, you know,

09:15.000 --> 09:21.000
and it's like, you know, think
about me having the, the leans,
how about the passengers who
aren't used to it.

09:21.000 --> 09:25.000
Because you can, not only can
you see out the, the left and
the right hand side, you can
look out in front,

09:25.000 --> 09:27.000
that's something you can't do on
a jetliner.

09:27.000 --> 09:34.000
So it's very confusing,
Christina was definitely, you
know, uneasy, which made me, of
course, all that more uneasy.

09:34.000 --> 09:38.000
But, you know, we've vectored
all the way down and it was, it
was awesome.

09:38.000 --> 09:41.000
I mean, doing it by hand,
there's, whoo, there's a lot to
it.

09:41.000 --> 09:48.000
Along with the GPS that slaves
to the autopilot, I say, but fun
to do, really, really fun to
just drop out of the clouds and
all of a sudden,

09:48.000 --> 09:51.000
boom, there it is, right at AM
Airport, right in front of you.

09:51.000 --> 09:53.000
And it's, and that's nicely lit.

09:53.000 --> 09:55.000
It's a huge airport where, you
know, big jets come in.

09:55.000 --> 10:01.000
Not 747s, but, you know,
vacation like 727s, I think 737s
can make it in.

10:01.000 --> 10:04.000
Nice long runway, and so, you
know, that was really good.

10:05.000 --> 10:12.000
And completed the landing and
went into the jet center,
because you can't just land at
the airport anymore.

10:12.000 --> 10:13.000
You have to have a handling
agent.

10:13.000 --> 10:17.000
So it costs like a 300 bucks to
fucking be handled.

10:17.000 --> 10:21.000
Which means they pick you up
from your plane with an
umbrella, take you into where
you pay,

10:21.000 --> 10:22.000
and then shut it out the back.

10:22.000 --> 10:25.000
And if you want, you can get a
cup of coffee, big deal.

10:25.000 --> 10:27.000
But didn't nice enough folks
there.

10:27.000 --> 10:29.000
So then we went to my mom's
house.

10:30.000 --> 10:34.000
It was exactly one year ago this
week that I was in Utrecht,

10:34.000 --> 10:38.000
for just before and just after
the holidays,

10:38.000 --> 10:42.000
where my mom was scheduled to
have a lung removed,

10:42.000 --> 10:45.000
due to lung cancer.

10:45.000 --> 10:48.000
And this was all podcasts back
in the day.

10:48.000 --> 10:50.000
Those of you who are just tuning
into the daily source code,

10:50.000 --> 10:52.000
and I was podcasting from the
hotel room.

10:52.000 --> 10:55.000
It was a weird, a very weird
Christmas.

10:55.000 --> 10:57.000
And she survived.

10:57.000 --> 11:00.000
You know, of the 15% of people
who survived that,

11:00.000 --> 11:03.000
and are declared cancer-free,
she made it.

11:03.000 --> 11:07.000
But throughout the year, she's
had another flare-up,

11:07.000 --> 11:10.000
but not in her, quote, crucial
organs, but in on her side,

11:10.000 --> 11:12.000
tumor, which was removed.

11:12.000 --> 11:15.000
And now there's some more cell
activity going on there.

11:15.000 --> 11:17.000
And if anything, it's fear.

11:17.000 --> 11:19.000
You know, that's what's really
freaking everybody out.

11:19.000 --> 11:20.000
It's the unknown.

11:20.000 --> 11:21.000
It's not knowing what we're at.

11:21.000 --> 11:23.000
What we're up against.

11:24.000 --> 11:28.000
And I must say, having all the
emails to support really,

11:28.000 --> 11:29.000
really helps.

11:29.000 --> 11:31.000
You feel all of a sudden you
feel a lot less alone.

11:31.000 --> 11:34.000
And you also realize how big
this problem is.

11:34.000 --> 11:36.000
It's so fucking medieval.

11:36.000 --> 11:40.000
That's what struck me this
morning when I woke up is,

11:40.000 --> 11:44.000
you know, to see someone sick,

11:44.000 --> 11:46.000
but actually, you know,

11:46.000 --> 11:49.000
I want to use the word wilting
away,

11:49.000 --> 11:50.000
but you can see it.

11:51.000 --> 11:53.000
It's literally eating at you.

11:53.000 --> 11:56.000
And it's like medieval, man.

11:56.000 --> 11:58.000
It's like the, you know,

11:58.000 --> 12:00.000
with all the technology we have,

12:00.000 --> 12:02.000
it's seen so unbelievable that
you,

12:02.000 --> 12:05.000
there's no fucking magic pill or
something.

12:05.000 --> 12:07.000
And you know, this is really the
only case

12:07.000 --> 12:10.000
in our immediate family and
friend, you know,

12:10.000 --> 12:13.000
surroundings that we've
witnessed this.

12:13.000 --> 12:17.000
And of course, it to be this
close is a major bummer.

12:18.000 --> 12:20.000
But, you know, mom's positive.

12:20.000 --> 12:22.000
She really is, but she's really
afraid.

12:22.000 --> 12:23.000
And she's tired of it.

12:23.000 --> 12:26.000
You know, she's tired of this
fighting the whole year.

12:26.000 --> 12:27.000
And now it just has to continue.

12:27.000 --> 12:30.000
So my sisters and I are,

12:30.000 --> 12:32.000
you know, going to go at it full
speed.

12:32.000 --> 12:33.000
Going to support her.

12:33.000 --> 12:35.000
And unfortunately, Tiffany,

12:35.000 --> 12:38.000
my oldest sister who was two
years younger than I am.

12:38.000 --> 12:39.000
She lives in Holland.

12:39.000 --> 12:40.000
So she, of course,

12:40.000 --> 12:42.000
is the closest in proximity.

12:42.000 --> 12:44.000
Willow, my younger sister,

12:44.000 --> 12:46.000
who was four years younger than
I.

12:46.000 --> 12:49.000
She lives in Italy.

12:49.000 --> 12:50.000
So, um,

12:50.000 --> 12:52.000
now, of course, I'm shuddling
back and forth

12:52.000 --> 12:53.000
between here in San Francisco.

12:53.000 --> 12:55.000
So I'm pretty sure that we're
going to get

12:55.000 --> 12:57.000
our instrument rating very
quickly.

12:57.000 --> 12:59.000
So that when I am back here,

12:59.000 --> 13:01.000
I can just make quick day trips
to go and see,

13:01.000 --> 13:02.000
see mom.

13:02.000 --> 13:03.000
Because the broader my airport
is really,

13:03.000 --> 13:05.000
it's very conveniently located

13:05.000 --> 13:06.000
to where she lives.

13:06.000 --> 13:07.000
Only half hour by cab.

13:07.000 --> 13:09.000
And actually, we called taxi
Eric.

13:09.000 --> 13:11.000
Tea to the E to come pick us up.

13:11.000 --> 13:13.000
And he was there last year when
my mom

13:13.000 --> 13:14.000
in the hospital.

13:14.000 --> 13:17.000
So it was a good, good hang.

13:17.000 --> 13:18.000
We had fun.

13:18.000 --> 13:19.000
We laughed.

13:19.000 --> 13:20.000
My sister came down.

13:20.000 --> 13:21.000
So that was a lot of fun.

13:21.000 --> 13:23.000
And Bob, my mom's a new husband.

13:23.000 --> 13:24.000
She got married to a couple of
years ago.

13:24.000 --> 13:27.000
Yeah, he, of course, took the
day off.

13:27.000 --> 13:28.000
And it was really good.

13:28.000 --> 13:29.000
It was just a couple hours.

13:29.000 --> 13:31.000
But it was, you know,

13:31.000 --> 13:33.000
enough to really raise her
spirits.

13:33.000 --> 13:36.000
And so that was cool.

13:36.000 --> 13:39.000
So humbled by all of that.

13:39.000 --> 13:41.000
Back to the airport.

13:41.000 --> 13:44.000
And I wanted to make it back
before dark.

13:44.000 --> 13:46.000
Now, of course, we were running
a little late.

13:46.000 --> 13:47.000
Now, this was not a problem.

13:47.000 --> 13:51.000
Even though the wind commander
couldn't come with me.

13:51.000 --> 13:56.000
We had Roy, who is actually my
IFR instrument instructor.

13:56.000 --> 13:58.000
And I'd never flown with Roy
before.

13:58.000 --> 14:01.000
You know, we'd done all kinds of
other ground classes together.

14:01.000 --> 14:05.000
But he is the absolute king of
instrument flying

14:05.000 --> 14:07.000
in the Netherlands and Belgium.

14:07.000 --> 14:08.000
He's just awesome.

14:08.000 --> 14:10.000
He's raided on everything, can
fly everything.

14:10.000 --> 14:11.000
And he's a nerd.

14:11.000 --> 14:16.000
He works like Ericsson or
something, doing something
really high-tech on the mobile
networks.

14:16.000 --> 14:17.000
I'm not quite sure.

14:17.000 --> 14:21.000
But, you know, he's got a flight
sim at home with multi-screens.

14:21.000 --> 14:23.000
So we flew home.

14:23.000 --> 14:24.000
It was beautiful.

14:24.000 --> 14:27.000
It was, you know, the sun was,
we were flying into the sunset,

14:27.000 --> 14:29.000
if you will.

14:29.000 --> 14:33.000
You know, just trying to
basically catch up with the sun

14:33.000 --> 14:34.000
as it was setting.

14:34.000 --> 14:35.000
It was so smooth sailing.

14:35.000 --> 14:37.000
It was like we were on a cable
car almost.

14:37.000 --> 14:38.000
It was so beautiful.

14:39.000 --> 14:43.000
Could trim the plane, you know,
perfectly hands off

14:43.000 --> 14:46.000
most of the time, just looking
at the surroundings,

14:46.000 --> 14:48.000
talking with Roy about stuff.

14:48.000 --> 14:49.000
So it was great.

14:49.000 --> 14:52.000
By the time we reached the coast
of Dover though,

14:52.000 --> 14:58.000
the sun had set and it was about
four PM local time.

14:58.000 --> 15:01.000
And we knew we're going to be
making an approach in the dark,

15:01.000 --> 15:03.000
which neither of us had ever
done at Ferrox,

15:03.000 --> 15:07.000
which has a reasonably short
strip, actually.

15:07.000 --> 15:09.000
And although they're open until
six,

15:09.000 --> 15:12.000
and they do have the runway lit.

15:12.000 --> 15:14.000
I think there's a difference
between a runway that's lit,

15:14.000 --> 15:17.000
you know, like having seen
Rotterdam airport with the
center line

15:17.000 --> 15:21.000
is a row of lights and there's
just lights everywhere.

15:21.000 --> 15:23.000
You know, it's a beautiful
sight.

15:23.000 --> 15:26.000
We actually passed over the
airport once

15:26.000 --> 15:28.000
because we couldn't even see it.

15:28.000 --> 15:30.000
Then we saw the runway.

15:30.000 --> 15:32.000
And it was like there had some
street lights on,

15:32.000 --> 15:34.000
casting some kind of glow onto
it.

15:34.000 --> 15:35.000
But, you know, no center line,

15:35.000 --> 15:37.000
just basically two rows of white
lights

15:37.000 --> 15:40.000
and land in the between there
somewhere, if you don't mind.

15:40.000 --> 15:42.000
And all of a sudden there was a
lot of activity

15:42.000 --> 15:44.000
there were people doing touching
goes

15:44.000 --> 15:46.000
and the dark people coming in
who were kind of doing the same
thing

15:46.000 --> 15:47.000
that we were doing.

15:47.000 --> 15:50.000
I would say probably one of the
most dangerous times

15:50.000 --> 15:52.000
at a small airfield or maybe any
airfield

15:52.000 --> 15:55.000
is around this time when people
are kind of rushing to get home

15:55.000 --> 15:57.000
or maybe they just got caught in
the dark.

15:57.000 --> 16:00.000
Now the experience, we had the
experience on board,

16:00.000 --> 16:03.000
so I was not worried at all.

16:04.000 --> 16:07.000
But we did, so we actually did
one go around

16:07.000 --> 16:09.000
as, you know, we were like,

16:09.000 --> 16:11.000
stirred in row for landing

16:11.000 --> 16:14.000
and the guy right in front of us
really

16:14.000 --> 16:15.000
was slow getting in.

16:15.000 --> 16:17.000
We were much, you know, much
faster aircraft

16:17.000 --> 16:18.000
and we were hanging everything
out

16:18.000 --> 16:19.000
to slow us down, gear down,

16:19.000 --> 16:21.000
flaps down, everything.

16:21.000 --> 16:23.000
And so we actually had to go
around

16:23.000 --> 16:25.000
because he wasn't getting off
the runway fast enough.

16:25.000 --> 16:26.000
So, you know, did another pass.

16:26.000 --> 16:28.000
That was actually good because
then I got

16:28.000 --> 16:30.000
a lot better feel for the
situation

16:30.000 --> 16:33.000
of where I was and I could
actually recall now

16:33.000 --> 16:35.000
because of course I've done
landings there.

16:35.000 --> 16:37.000
You know, what the base and the
final leg

16:37.000 --> 16:41.000
should feel like still came in
high.

16:41.000 --> 16:43.000
Which is really cool because
even though

